WEBVTT

00:00.900 --> 00:04.067
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.108 --> 00:07.360
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:07.394 --> 00:10.946
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:10.981 --> 00:13.933
♪ On which we used to rely?

00:13.967 --> 00:17.451
♪ Lucky there's a family guy

00:17.485 --> 00:20.739
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:20.773 --> 00:22.307
♪ All the things that make us

00:22.341 --> 00:23.989
♪ Laugh and cry

00:24.023 --> 00:26.994
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:35.684 --> 00:37.635
Boy, what a great day
for a baseball game.

00:37.668 --> 00:38.969
Yeah, and these
are the best seats

00:39.004 --> 00:40.821
in the house
for catching home run balls.

00:40.856 --> 00:42.087
Aw, that'd be sweet.

00:42.122 --> 00:44.956
The only souvenir I ever got was
at that stock car race.

00:51.980 --> 00:53.947
All right,
I got the guy!

00:53.981 --> 00:55.132
I got the guy.

00:57.784 --> 00:59.251
Man, I hate
the Yankees.

00:59.286 --> 01:01.169
Yeah! You are all villains
to me

01:01.203 --> 01:04.723
because I happen to live in this
region of the country. Yeah!

01:04.757 --> 01:05.725
(crowd cheering)

01:05.742 --> 01:07.724
What's going on?
Did I miss something?

01:07.742 --> 01:09.860
Nah, just some idiot streaking
across the field.

01:09.894 --> 01:13.329
Ha ha. You're all looking
at my penis.

01:13.363 --> 01:16.681
You didn't plan on it,
but it's happening.

01:16.716 --> 01:19.433
Oh, cool. Two
spots opened up.

01:19.468 --> 01:21.368
You, uh-- y-you go ahead.

01:21.403 --> 01:23.537
I can wait. I might--
I might wait for a stall.

01:23.572 --> 01:24.872
Oh, come on, Peter.

01:24.906 --> 01:27.156
We're missing the game.

01:27.190 --> 01:28.775
(unzipping)

01:28.809 --> 01:29.776
Hey. Hi.

01:29.810 --> 01:31.176
Hey.

01:31.211 --> 01:33.178
<i>PETER: Damn it.
He already started.</i>

01:33.213 --> 01:35.680
<i>If I don't start peeing soon,
it's gonna be weird.</i>

01:35.714 --> 01:37.682
<i>He'll be all done,
and I'll still be here.</i>

01:37.716 --> 01:38.748
<i>Come on, Peter. Pee.</i>

01:38.782 --> 01:40.416
<i>Your dad fought
in World War II,</i>

01:40.450 --> 01:42.086
<i>and you can't even pee
in a urinal?</i>

01:42.121 --> 01:43.587
<i>What the hell's
wrong with you?</i>

01:43.621 --> 01:44.853
All set?
Yup.

01:44.887 --> 01:45.854
(zipping)

01:45.888 --> 01:47.190
I know you didn't go.

01:47.225 --> 01:49.792
I'm'a go pee
in a stairwell.

01:49.826 --> 01:52.024
STADIUM ANNOUNCER: Now batting
for the Red Sox,

01:52.059 --> 01:54.562
number 34, David Ortiz.

01:54.597 --> 01:56.029
Come on, Big Papi!

01:56.064 --> 01:57.864
(crack of bat, crowd cheering)

01:57.898 --> 01:59.364
It's coming straight for us!

01:59.398 --> 02:00.983
I got it, I got it!

02:01.968 --> 02:03.801
Mine! I got it!
It's my ball

02:03.835 --> 02:05.636
Damn it, Brian!
That came to me!

02:05.670 --> 02:07.404
That's my ball!

02:07.439 --> 02:08.807
(wind whistling)
What?

02:08.841 --> 02:10.405
Holy crap!

02:10.440 --> 02:14.244
(crowd murmuring)
(gasps)

02:14.278 --> 02:16.377
(crowd laughing hysterically)

02:16.411 --> 02:20.248
(slowly increasing
horrified scream)

02:20.282 --> 02:21.916
Wow, I guess
all this time,

02:21.950 --> 02:23.917
Quagmire should've
been saying "wiggity."

02:23.951 --> 02:26.253
Ha!
No, but he'sour friend.

02:30.356 --> 02:31.657
<i>TV ANNOUNCER: And now it's time</i>

02:31.691 --> 02:33.159
<i>for
the Channel Five Sports Report</i>

02:33.193 --> 02:34.492
<i>brought to you by Kia.</i>

02:34.527 --> 02:36.460
<i>Kia: Too bad it's a Kia.</i>

02:36.494 --> 02:37.960
A big day at Fenway today,

02:37.995 --> 02:40.465
as the Red Sox bested
the Yankees seven to six.

02:40.499 --> 02:42.130
But what really
has everyone talking

02:42.166 --> 02:44.735
is the fan who lost his toupee
at the game.

02:44.769 --> 02:46.369
The hilarious video already has

02:46.403 --> 02:48.470
over two million views
on YouTube.

02:48.504 --> 02:51.505
Comments ranged from sympathy
all the way to racial slurs

02:51.540 --> 02:53.308
directed at others
who chose to comment.

02:53.342 --> 02:55.441
Great, I'm a
laughingstock.

02:55.475 --> 02:56.442
What do I do?

02:56.477 --> 02:58.012
Come on, Quagmire.
It's not so bad.

02:58.046 --> 02:59.628
You could kill yourself.

02:59.662 --> 03:01.063
Well, it's just a thought,

03:01.097 --> 03:03.565
but have you ever considered
just embracing your baldness?

03:03.600 --> 03:05.133
Yeah, Brian's right.

03:05.168 --> 03:07.132
You know, there's lots of
big Hollywood celebrities

03:07.167 --> 03:08.803
who have gone bald with dignity.

03:08.837 --> 03:11.739
John Travolta, David Spade,
Nicolas Cage, Jeremy Piven...

03:11.773 --> 03:13.087
And besides,
don't you think

03:13.122 --> 03:15.308
you'll be happier just
being who you really are?

03:15.343 --> 03:17.258
(sighing): Maybe you're
right. What the hell.

03:17.294 --> 03:19.327
I mean, wh-what other choice do
I have at this point?

03:19.361 --> 03:20.694
Everybody knows
now anyway.

03:20.728 --> 03:21.679
Great.
Trust me.

03:21.714 --> 03:23.463
Nobody's gonna give
you a hard time.

03:23.498 --> 03:25.198
And even if they do,
just ignore them.

03:25.232 --> 03:26.966
That's what I do
with that weekend bully.

03:27.000 --> 03:28.968
Hey, your weekend sucks!

03:29.002 --> 03:30.736
I've already
gone on a hike

03:30.771 --> 03:33.004
and fixed a few things
around my house!

03:33.039 --> 03:34.104
What are you doing?!

03:34.139 --> 03:35.423
Peter, who is that man?

03:35.458 --> 03:36.941
I don't want to talk
about it.

03:39.811 --> 03:42.847
Dad, there's a weird bald guy
out in front of our house.

03:42.881 --> 03:44.161
Ah, that's just Quagmire.

03:44.196 --> 03:46.265
He's acting all different
now that he's bald.

03:46.299 --> 03:47.299
I'll go talk to him.

03:48.334 --> 03:49.800
Hey, slow down!

03:49.835 --> 03:51.468
Drive like hell,
you'll get there!

03:51.502 --> 03:53.303
Quagmire, what the
hell are you doing?

03:53.337 --> 03:55.305
I'm just letting
all these hot-rodders know

03:55.339 --> 03:57.991
that this is a neighborhood,
not a speedway track.

03:58.025 --> 03:59.325
Too fast, too fast!

03:59.359 --> 04:01.461
Our children should not have
to live in fear!

04:01.495 --> 04:03.261
Okay, well, how come
you're not doing this

04:03.295 --> 04:04.696
in front of your own house?

04:04.730 --> 04:06.681
Oh, my ham radio interferes
with the radar gun.

04:06.715 --> 04:08.866
Talked to a fella in Papua
New Guinea last night.

04:08.900 --> 04:11.032
You should come by sometime
and join in the fun.

04:11.067 --> 04:12.703
VOICE ON RADIO: Hello?

04:12.737 --> 04:14.037
H-Hello?

04:14.072 --> 04:15.870
Are you bald?
(static crackles)

04:15.904 --> 04:17.239
Yeah.

04:20.877 --> 04:21.842
Can I help you?

04:21.877 --> 04:23.509
Well, I hope you can, Sharon.

04:23.543 --> 04:26.512
<i>I'd just like to return this
VHS copy of Pete's Dragon.</i>

04:26.546 --> 04:29.515
Watched, enjoyed
and rewound.

04:29.550 --> 04:31.516
Uh, there are a lot
of people waiting.

04:31.551 --> 04:33.186
Is there anything else?

04:33.220 --> 04:35.236
Uh, yes. Do you have
a garbage can back there?

04:35.270 --> 04:38.456
I have the backings to some
Fruit Roll-Ups I need to discard.

04:38.490 --> 04:40.656
I'm sorry. That's for
library trash only.

04:40.690 --> 04:42.308
I understand.
That's okay.

04:42.342 --> 04:44.660
I'll just hold on to these
for the rest of the day.

04:44.694 --> 04:46.662
It ain't easy
bein' green, huh? (weak chuckle)

04:46.696 --> 04:48.196
That's from a song.

04:48.230 --> 04:49.197
Is that all?

04:49.231 --> 04:50.833
Yeah. Is there
an age restriction

04:50.867 --> 04:53.166
for the sleight-of-hand
magic show this afternoon?

04:53.201 --> 04:54.767
Twelve.
Ooh.

04:54.801 --> 04:57.337
Maybe I'll just stand
in the hallway and look in.

04:57.372 --> 05:00.004
Now, we all saw you put
your card back in the deck.

05:00.038 --> 05:03.208
So, how did it end up...
in your shirt pocket?

05:03.242 --> 05:06.944
Oh, my God,
how did he do that?

05:09.915 --> 05:12.281
Oh, no, there's Quagmire.

05:13.516 --> 05:14.984
He's been so
weird lately.

05:15.019 --> 05:16.587
You notice now
that he's bald,

05:16.621 --> 05:19.102
whenever he thinks about something,
he scratches his wrist?

05:19.136 --> 05:20.772
Hey, Quagmire,
we got a pitcher.

05:20.807 --> 05:21.772
You want a beer?

05:21.808 --> 05:23.157
Ehh, I don't think so.

05:23.191 --> 05:25.292
I was planning on looking
at pictures of birds later,

05:25.326 --> 05:27.177
and I don't want to be all
fuzzy-headed for that.

05:27.211 --> 05:29.180
Listen, Quagmire,
we hate you now.

05:29.214 --> 05:30.511
Yeah, you used to be fun.

05:30.546 --> 05:32.866
Yeah, now you're always either
preparing for a colonoscopy

05:32.900 --> 05:34.515
or just coming back
from a colonoscopy.

05:34.550 --> 05:36.185
The (bleep) going on
down there?

05:36.219 --> 05:38.185
Well, that's what we're
trying to figure out.

05:38.219 --> 05:39.869
Come on, we want
our buddy back.

05:39.887 --> 05:41.521
I'm not putting that
toupee on again, Peter.

05:41.556 --> 05:43.955
So... I-I'm not sure
what you want me to do.

05:43.990 --> 05:45.626
You could get
a hair transplant.

05:45.660 --> 05:48.477
Oh, yeah. Like one of those guys
pretending to get out of the pool.

05:48.512 --> 05:49.760
That looks all right.

05:49.794 --> 05:51.662
Yeah, come on, Quagmire.
Get the hair transplant.

05:51.696 --> 05:52.663
Come back to us.

05:52.697 --> 05:54.548
You're right.
This isn't me.

05:54.582 --> 05:56.883
Baldness is for women's
crotches, not men's heads.

05:56.917 --> 05:58.984
There you go,
that's pretty gross.

05:59.018 --> 06:00.653
Yeah, that's the spirit,
Quagmire.

06:00.688 --> 06:02.736
Oh, boy, listen, thanks
for putting up with me

06:02.770 --> 06:04.406
the last couple weeks.
Hey, no problem.

06:04.440 --> 06:06.892
You put up with me
when I was barefoot guy.

06:06.926 --> 06:09.409
Boy, nothing like a beer
with the fellas, eh?

06:09.443 --> 06:11.845
Peter, would you mind
putting on some shoes?

06:11.879 --> 06:13.179
Oh, you mean
foot prisons?

06:13.214 --> 06:14.214
Yes, I would.

06:17.250 --> 06:19.451
Glenn Quagmire?

06:19.486 --> 06:21.051
Why does that name
sound familiar?

06:21.085 --> 06:22.569
Oh, yeah, I treated
you for butt flu.

06:22.603 --> 06:23.953
Hey, you want
to keep it down?

06:23.987 --> 06:25.472
Easy now. That's
my privilege

06:25.507 --> 06:27.506
as a doctor to talk
about that stuff.

06:29.475 --> 06:31.143
How's it going,
Dr. Kamada?

06:31.161 --> 06:33.160
Bob!

06:34.162 --> 06:35.931
Mattaaayy.

06:35.966 --> 06:37.748
Oh, hiya, Rachael.

06:37.783 --> 06:39.416
Oh, hey, Dr. Geller.

06:39.451 --> 06:40.750
Sarah.

06:40.784 --> 06:42.418
Jim.

06:42.453 --> 06:43.920
Merianna.

06:43.954 --> 06:45.088
Geez, Joe.

06:45.122 --> 06:46.521
Yeah, I come here a lot.

06:46.556 --> 06:47.855
All right, I'm gonna
hit the john.

06:47.889 --> 06:50.590
Good. Me and Joe can stay here
and tell secrets.

06:50.625 --> 06:53.126
I'm wearing my dead
grandmother's panties.

06:53.160 --> 06:56.096
DOCTOR: I'm sorry
to have to tell you this,

06:56.131 --> 06:58.680
Mr. Pewterschmidt,
but the cancer is terminal.

06:58.714 --> 07:00.800
If you're lucky,
you've got two weeks to live.

07:00.835 --> 07:02.668
I understand.

07:02.702 --> 07:03.936
Oh, my God!

07:03.971 --> 07:05.936
Carter's dying?!

07:05.970 --> 07:09.874
I now declare this
hair transplant officially over!

07:09.908 --> 07:10.856
(fires gun)

07:10.891 --> 07:12.024
Hey, hey, hey!

07:12.059 --> 07:13.025
He's back!

07:13.060 --> 07:14.811
You look great,
Quagmire.

07:14.846 --> 07:16.278
(pager beeping)

07:16.313 --> 07:19.046
Oh, my God. One of my patients
on Two has been shot.

07:24.733 --> 07:25.646
Hey, Stewie.

07:25.647 --> 07:26.947
Um, hey, what's up?

07:26.982 --> 07:29.250
Were you-- were you just
looking at Rollerblades?

07:29.284 --> 07:30.583
What? No.
Yeah, who cares?

07:30.617 --> 07:31.618
What do you want?

07:31.652 --> 07:33.404
Listen, I was at the hospital
earlier,

07:33.438 --> 07:35.639
and I heard something
I wish I hadn't.

07:35.673 --> 07:38.542
Oh, please do not tell me
it was a sexist remark,

07:38.576 --> 07:40.542
because... because
that is not okay.

07:40.576 --> 07:42.876
You know, those nurses
are busting their asses,

07:42.911 --> 07:44.978
weighing people and
seeing how tall they are.

07:45.013 --> 07:48.614
Stewie, I heard a doctor tell
Carter he has two weeks to live.

07:48.649 --> 07:50.348
What? Are you sure?

07:50.383 --> 07:51.850
I saw it with my own eyes.

07:51.884 --> 07:53.753
I gotta check this out.

07:53.804 --> 07:55.569
Oh, my God, you're right!

07:55.604 --> 07:56.788
He looks awful.

07:56.823 --> 07:58.990
(beeping)

07:59.025 --> 08:00.325
Wait, how are
we seeing this?

08:00.359 --> 08:02.692
Oh, I've got a nanny cam
hidden inside his house.

08:02.726 --> 08:04.296
Watching him nail his
cleaning woman is how

08:04.321 --> 08:05.728
I'm learning Spanish.

08:05.729 --> 08:08.261
Although I'm not sure what
the Spanish word "no" means,

08:08.296 --> 08:10.799
because it doesn't seem to mean
the same thing as it does here.

08:10.834 --> 08:13.433
And the worst part is, I don't
think Lois even knows he's sick.

08:13.467 --> 08:15.669
Oh, this is going
to destroy her!

08:15.703 --> 08:18.354
Oh, just thinking about
it makes me all giddy.

08:18.388 --> 08:20.422
Like a group of women
ordering dessert.

08:20.457 --> 08:22.591
And then we have a chocolate,
chocolate cake

08:22.626 --> 08:25.110
that's infused with chocolate served
with chocolate ice cream

08:25.162 --> 08:26.610
and a molten
chocolate topping.

08:26.661 --> 08:28.531
We'll have one of those
with four spoons.

08:28.565 --> 08:31.115
More like four of those
with one spoon for me!

08:31.167 --> 08:34.150
(continuous, staccato,
giddy laughter)

08:42.391 --> 08:44.359
Oh, hi, Brian.

08:44.393 --> 08:45.692
Hey, Lois.

08:45.726 --> 08:48.946
Listen, I have something
I need to tell you, but...

08:48.980 --> 08:50.446
it might be difficult for you
to hear.

08:50.481 --> 08:51.513
What is it?

08:51.548 --> 08:52.848
Lois...

08:52.882 --> 08:54.682
I think your father is dying.

08:54.716 --> 08:56.251
What?!
(flashbulb pops)

08:56.269 --> 08:57.552
Ha!

08:57.586 --> 08:59.387
Brian, what are you
talking about?

08:59.421 --> 09:01.021
Well, yesterday at the hospital,

09:01.055 --> 09:03.590
I heard a doctor tell him
he has cancer...

09:03.623 --> 09:05.524
and that he has
two weeks to live.

09:05.559 --> 09:06.958
Oh, my God.

09:06.992 --> 09:08.694
Oh, my God, Daddy!

09:08.728 --> 09:10.528
Yeah, I feel really
horrible for you,

09:10.563 --> 09:12.095
and I hate to do this,

09:12.129 --> 09:13.799
but I'm trying to
catch a 4:50 movie,

09:13.833 --> 09:16.566
and it's, like, 4:40 now, and,
so, if I'm going to make it,

09:16.600 --> 09:18.601
I have to leave,
like, now.

09:22.538 --> 09:24.506
Yeah, this-this stinks,

09:24.540 --> 09:27.709
but I've already blown this guy off,
like, twice, so...

09:31.962 --> 09:34.114
I've been trying to call
my parents all day,

09:34.132 --> 09:35.830
and I can't get
ahold of them.

09:35.881 --> 09:38.000
I'll tell ya, this
obituary came out great.

09:38.051 --> 09:41.252
"Carter Pewterschmidt died
today of the disease cancer.

09:41.286 --> 09:43.003
"In lieu of flowers,
please send

09:43.054 --> 09:46.289
carnival rides to Peter Griffin
at 31 Spooner Street."

09:46.307 --> 09:48.090
If we get just one
ride out of this,

09:48.124 --> 09:49.658
it will have
been a success.

09:54.296 --> 09:55.263
Lois?

09:55.297 --> 09:56.665
Wh-What are you
doing here?

09:56.699 --> 09:58.967
Mom, I've been trying
to get ahold of you.

09:59.001 --> 10:00.700
Is Daddy okay?
Where is he?

10:00.734 --> 10:02.501
Oh, he's fine, dear.

10:02.536 --> 10:04.004
But now's not
a good time.

10:04.038 --> 10:05.422
He's very busy.

10:05.456 --> 10:07.155
Look, I know
something's going on.

10:07.189 --> 10:08.290
I want to see him.

10:10.594 --> 10:11.826
Daddy?!

10:11.861 --> 10:14.611
Well, look who's here!

10:14.645 --> 10:16.598
Daddy! You're okay!

10:16.632 --> 10:18.165
Of course I'm okay!
Why wouldn't I be?

10:18.199 --> 10:20.500
Well, Brian said
you were dying!

10:20.534 --> 10:21.701
And I finished a book!

10:21.735 --> 10:23.869
I promised myself
I'd work that in.

10:23.903 --> 10:25.870
I-It's-- I might've
jumped the gun.

10:25.904 --> 10:27.004
I didn't finish it.

10:27.039 --> 10:29.340
I'm so glad
you're all right.

10:29.374 --> 10:32.057
Brian, how could you get us
all worked up like that?!

10:32.092 --> 10:33.525
Yeah, shame on you!

10:33.561 --> 10:35.127
Oh, you're gonna
get it, Brian.

10:35.161 --> 10:37.497
You are gonna get it
when I drink.

10:38.163 --> 10:39.781
What the hell's going on?

10:39.815 --> 10:41.382
Last night, Carter was on
his deathbed,

10:41.418 --> 10:43.067
and now he looks better
than he ever did.

10:43.102 --> 10:44.885
Well, they say laughter
is the best medicine.

10:44.919 --> 10:46.854
Maybe he went to a
comedy club or something.

10:46.889 --> 10:48.205
(audience laughing)

10:48.239 --> 10:49.239
In my neighborhood,

10:49.275 --> 10:50.739
we didn't have no Kool-Aid.

10:50.773 --> 10:52.524
We had food colorin' and water.

10:52.558 --> 10:54.593
Mama used to call it
"Fool-Aid."

10:54.628 --> 10:56.592
(laughter)
CARTER: No, I don't get that.

10:56.627 --> 10:58.262
And everybody had an Uncle Earl.

10:58.296 --> 11:00.263
You know, that uncle
that sit around in the house

11:00.298 --> 11:03.067
all day in his underdrawers,
saying he "between" jobs.

11:03.101 --> 11:04.399
To be between jobs,

11:04.434 --> 11:06.637
you had to have had a job
in the first place!

11:06.671 --> 11:07.737
CARTER:
That's accurate!

11:07.771 --> 11:09.570
And Earl got that cell phone.

11:09.604 --> 11:11.038
He on the Sprint plan.

11:11.073 --> 11:12.505
When the bill come, he sprint.

11:12.540 --> 11:15.509
CARTER: Yes! So many of them
are delinquent in payment.

11:19.680 --> 11:21.347
Rupert, move your legs.

11:21.381 --> 11:22.647
I can see your balls.

11:22.681 --> 11:24.633
Stewie, this is
going to sound crazy,

11:24.668 --> 11:27.036
but I have a theory about
what's going on with Carter.

11:27.070 --> 11:28.068
Really? What is it?

11:28.102 --> 11:29.135
Well, think about it.

11:29.170 --> 11:30.437
If Carter Pewterschmidt,

11:30.471 --> 11:32.339
the dynamic chairman of
Pewterschmidt Industries,

11:32.373 --> 11:34.074
were to fall ill
or even die,

11:34.108 --> 11:35.640
the stock price
would plummet.

11:35.675 --> 11:37.509
I don't want you to think
I'm not listening,

11:37.543 --> 11:39.194
but when's Christmas?
Not for a while.

11:39.229 --> 11:41.278
But what I'm saying is,
I think Carter is sick

11:41.313 --> 11:44.114
or possibly even dead,
and that the man we saw today

11:44.149 --> 11:45.849
was an imposter
brought in to keep

11:45.884 --> 11:47.717
Pewterschmidt Industries'
stock price up

11:47.751 --> 11:49.720
and to keep people
from knowing the truth.

11:49.754 --> 11:51.387
Boy, you are just
going to keep on talking

11:51.422 --> 11:53.221
with that poppy seed
stuck in your teeth, aren't you?

11:53.256 --> 11:54.488
Anyway, what are you
suggesting?

11:54.523 --> 11:56.173
We can follow him, see
if he's really Carter.

11:56.208 --> 11:58.077
Okay, but I got to be
home by 7:00.

11:58.112 --> 11:59.442
I'm supposed to talk
to the police

11:59.477 --> 12:00.744
about that shooting
I witnessed.

12:03.214 --> 12:05.249
Go Lord, are
you all right?

12:05.283 --> 12:06.548
If I die,

12:06.583 --> 12:09.585
leave two dollars
worth of flowers right here.

12:09.619 --> 12:12.202
Here's my fourth grade
class photo.

12:12.236 --> 12:14.237
Lean it against
a cheap candle.

12:14.272 --> 12:15.773
Well, should I call
an ambulance?

12:15.808 --> 12:17.574
No. Call Channel Nine.

12:17.608 --> 12:20.475
And have a gap-toothed
woman say, quote,

12:20.509 --> 12:22.945
"He never bothered nobody."

12:26.282 --> 12:28.782
Okay, if this guy is an
imposter, he's going to have

12:28.818 --> 12:30.151
to do everything
Carter normally does.

12:30.186 --> 12:32.585
We just have to wait
and see if he slips up.

12:32.619 --> 12:33.953
All right,
here he comes now.

12:39.993 --> 12:41.593
All right, Stu, now,
if we can just...

12:41.611 --> 12:42.778
No.
What?

12:42.812 --> 12:44.259
You know what you did.

12:44.277 --> 12:45.762
Hey, wait a minute.

12:45.780 --> 12:46.946
If this is an imposter,

12:46.982 --> 12:48.699
then this guy we're following
never had cancer.

12:48.734 --> 12:50.032
Yeah, so?

12:50.066 --> 12:52.118
Well, you're a dog.
Can't you just sniff cancer?

12:52.153 --> 12:53.619
No, that's just a
myth we started

12:53.653 --> 12:56.054
so we could stick our
snouts in girls' boobs.

12:56.088 --> 12:58.155
It's nice to have a
clean bill of health.

12:58.189 --> 12:59.290
Well, for now.

12:59.324 --> 13:00.992
The key is
weekly follow-ups.

13:01.026 --> 13:03.659
You know, cervical cancer
is also a problem.

13:03.693 --> 13:05.862
My boyfriend thinks he
might have prostate cancer.

13:05.897 --> 13:07.229
Nope, doesn't.

13:12.835 --> 13:14.418
Okay, now we've got him.

13:14.452 --> 13:16.720
The real Carter would
never go to a Starbucks.

13:16.755 --> 13:19.323
He thinks they're too
liberal and pretentious.

13:21.107 --> 13:22.674
Coffee for Brent.

13:22.708 --> 13:24.209
(snickering)

13:24.243 --> 13:26.529
Latte for Cecil.

13:26.563 --> 13:28.161
(snickering)

13:28.195 --> 13:29.664
Beanie?

13:29.699 --> 13:32.016
(laughs) None of those
people are real.

13:32.049 --> 13:34.800
I'm stealing all your
sugar packets and napkins.

13:34.834 --> 13:37.670
Woodstock's over, (bleep).

13:48.029 --> 13:50.680
Wow. You certainly
look a lot better

13:50.715 --> 13:52.312
than that walking corpse
I saw last week.

13:52.347 --> 13:54.182
Well, no one's ever
going to see that again,

13:54.216 --> 13:55.517
I can promise you that.

13:55.551 --> 13:57.285
I can't stress how
important it is

13:57.319 --> 13:58.820
our secret be kept
from the public.

13:58.854 --> 14:00.052
I understand, sir.

14:00.087 --> 14:02.290
And I assure you
that Specimen Z

14:02.324 --> 14:04.258
is secure on level 12.

14:04.292 --> 14:05.557
Did you hear that, Brian?

14:05.591 --> 14:08.292
That "walking corpse" was
Carter dying of cancer.

14:08.326 --> 14:09.995
This guy is an imposter.

14:10.029 --> 14:11.396
Oh, my God.

14:11.431 --> 14:14.165
And I bet Specimen Z
is the real Carter's body.

14:14.199 --> 14:15.666
You two didn't
hear that, did you?

14:15.700 --> 14:16.767
BRIAN:
Hey, buddy, we're...

14:16.802 --> 14:18.400
we're just trying to
read the paper here.

14:18.435 --> 14:20.468
I'll have you fired
for talking to me like that.

14:20.502 --> 14:21.638
I don't work here.

14:21.672 --> 14:23.540
Then I'll have you killed.
I want to die.

14:24.573 --> 14:26.541
I hated that guy back there.

14:29.645 --> 14:31.794
Okay, this is level 12.

14:31.828 --> 14:33.045
There's the lab.

14:33.079 --> 14:34.948
Let's get in there
and find Carter's body.

14:34.982 --> 14:36.348
Oh, crap, there's a keypad.

14:36.383 --> 14:38.199
All right, we're just
going to have to try

14:38.234 --> 14:39.367
and figure out the code.

14:39.401 --> 14:40.536
When's his birthday?

14:40.570 --> 14:42.119
I think it's in March.

14:42.153 --> 14:43.437
Stewie, are you crazy?

14:43.471 --> 14:45.039
That's so loud.
The guards will hear.

14:45.074 --> 14:46.256
Don't worry
about it, Bri...

14:46.291 --> 14:48.092
MAN: Hey, what's
going on down there?

14:48.127 --> 14:49.658
Nothing.

14:49.692 --> 14:51.194
All right.

14:51.228 --> 14:52.878
All right, now,
where would they keep

14:52.913 --> 14:55.296
a dead body
in a pharmaceutical lab?

14:55.330 --> 14:57.047
(gasps) Brian, look.

14:58.350 --> 15:00.668
That's it.
I bet he's in there.

15:09.992 --> 15:11.860
Oh, my God.

15:11.895 --> 15:14.327
Specimen Z isn't Carter's body.

15:14.362 --> 15:15.512
What is it?

15:15.546 --> 15:18.248
Specimen Z is a
cure for cancer.

15:20.716 --> 15:23.019
Carter didn't die.
He cured himself.

15:23.054 --> 15:24.870
CARTER: Hold it right there.
(both gasp)

15:24.905 --> 15:26.604
Damn it, Brian.
You broke into my lab.

15:26.639 --> 15:28.172
And you didn't wear a suit.

15:28.206 --> 15:30.607
Hey, why didn't he
have to wear a suit?

15:30.642 --> 15:32.042
See what you've started now?

15:37.095 --> 15:39.662
Carter, you've found
a cure for cancer?

15:39.696 --> 15:41.097
Well, I didn't come up with it.

15:41.130 --> 15:42.531
A couple of eggheads
I hired did.

15:42.566 --> 15:44.434
One of them's a Chinese.

15:44.468 --> 15:46.034
Boy, I don't like those people.

15:46.068 --> 15:48.735
But holy crap, wind 'em up
and watch 'em go.

15:48.770 --> 15:50.103
How long have you had this?

15:50.138 --> 15:51.672
I don't know.
When... when was that

15:51.706 --> 15:53.039
"Who Let the Dogs Out?" song?

15:53.073 --> 15:54.908
You've had this since 1999?

15:54.943 --> 15:57.410
You know when "Who Let
the Dogs Out?" came out?

15:57.444 --> 15:59.444
It's a song about dogs
and letting them out.

15:59.478 --> 16:01.714
I could have you arrested
for breaking in here.

16:01.748 --> 16:04.266
Carter, you've discovered the
holy grail of modern medicine.

16:04.300 --> 16:06.449
Why the hell would you
keep it buried like this?

16:06.484 --> 16:07.534
I'll tell you why.

16:07.569 --> 16:09.204
Because there's
far more money to be made

16:09.238 --> 16:11.003
in treating a disease
than in curing it.

16:11.037 --> 16:12.839
Why cure someone
of cancer in a day

16:12.873 --> 16:14.540
if we can treat them
for a lifetime

16:14.575 --> 16:16.242
and bill them
every step along the way?

16:16.276 --> 16:17.741
What? That's insane.

16:17.776 --> 16:20.228
Brian, it's so cold in here,
look how big my nips are.

16:20.262 --> 16:21.997
They're almost like a woman's.

16:22.031 --> 16:25.616
Oh, look, I'm tugging at 'em
and they're getting even bigger.

16:25.651 --> 16:27.082
Carter, what you're
doing here is criminal,

16:27.117 --> 16:29.201
and I'm going to tell the
whole world about it.

16:29.236 --> 16:30.835
Is that right?
Who's going to believe you?

16:30.869 --> 16:32.070
The Internet?

16:32.104 --> 16:34.372
You'll be just another nut-job,
left-wing blogger.

16:34.406 --> 16:35.739
Security!

16:35.773 --> 16:36.907
MAN:
Everything's fine.

16:36.941 --> 16:38.276
No, it isn't. Get in here.

16:39.944 --> 16:41.794
Ah! Damn it! You bastards.

16:41.845 --> 16:43.813
I don't know why you're
surprised by this, Brian.

16:43.846 --> 16:45.648
Big corporations
never tell the truth.

16:45.682 --> 16:47.716
ok at the
Kleenex industry.

16:47.750 --> 16:49.083
Well, we had another
good quarter.

16:49.117 --> 16:51.285
I guess a lot of people
are sneezing out there.

16:51.319 --> 16:53.153
Jerry, I'm going to let you
in on something,

16:53.188 --> 16:54.921
and it can't
leave this room.

16:54.954 --> 16:56.089
Our research shows

16:56.123 --> 16:58.324
the primary use
of our product is sperm.

16:58.359 --> 16:59.459
What?

16:59.493 --> 17:01.025
Yeah, and brace
yourself for this.

17:01.059 --> 17:03.827
The second biggest use
is women crying about nonsense.

17:03.861 --> 17:05.330
But I thought
sneezing was...

17:05.364 --> 17:08.281
Jerry, Jerry,
it's sperm and nonsense.

17:10.735 --> 17:11.870
I can't believe Carter

17:11.904 --> 17:13.618
would sacrifice
millions of lives

17:13.652 --> 17:15.120
just to protect
corporate profits.

17:15.155 --> 17:16.453
Well, don't be too upset.

17:16.487 --> 17:17.740
Old Stewie here snagged

17:17.774 --> 17:19.624
a little souvenir
on his way out.

17:19.658 --> 17:21.041
Oh, my God, Stewie.

17:21.075 --> 17:22.276
You got the cancer cure.

17:22.294 --> 17:23.660
How'd you manage to steal it?

17:23.711 --> 17:25.295
Oh, it was easy--
I just took it

17:25.330 --> 17:26.711
while you guys
were yammering on.

17:26.746 --> 17:28.465
I swear, I felt
like I was waiting

17:28.499 --> 17:30.082
for Lois to get
off the phone.

17:30.116 --> 17:31.751
Well, that is amazing.

17:31.785 --> 17:32.950
All right, Bonnie.

17:32.984 --> 17:34.618
Well, it was good
talking to you.

17:34.653 --> 17:36.588
No way. She did?

17:36.622 --> 17:38.187
When did she say that?

17:38.222 --> 17:39.455
You know what?

17:39.490 --> 17:42.358
That doesn't surprise me.
That's how she is.

17:42.393 --> 17:44.626
Okay, then. Okay.

17:44.661 --> 17:46.727
Okay. Okay.

17:46.762 --> 17:48.664
All right,
I'll see you Tuesday.

17:48.698 --> 17:51.715
Oh, no. What happened?

17:51.749 --> 17:53.517
(gasps) That's terrible.

17:53.552 --> 17:56.152
Oh, my God, just awful.

17:56.187 --> 17:58.019
Well, you
give them my best.

17:58.053 --> 17:59.488
Okay, I got to go.

17:59.522 --> 18:01.558
Stewie and I are headed
out to the beach.

18:01.592 --> 18:03.124
Oh, I didn't tell you?

18:03.158 --> 18:04.759
He's learning to swim.

18:04.793 --> 18:07.095
Oh, I got him
the cutest swim trunks.

18:07.129 --> 18:08.546
At Kohl's.

18:08.580 --> 18:10.479
You've never
been to Kohl's?

18:10.513 --> 18:12.481
Well, let me tell you
about Kohl's.

18:12.516 --> 18:13.749
(sighs)

18:13.784 --> 18:15.933
All right, well, you know
the mall with the Chili's?

18:15.967 --> 18:17.369
It's across the street.

18:17.403 --> 18:18.571
(gunshot, Bonnie shrieks)

18:18.605 --> 18:20.071
Bonnie?
Bonnie, are you there?

18:20.106 --> 18:22.006
What happened?
STEWIE: Let's go!

18:25.042 --> 18:26.843
Oh, there you two are.

18:26.877 --> 18:28.111
Lois, there's
something incredible

18:28.145 --> 18:29.277
I have to tell you.

18:29.312 --> 18:30.412
And there's
something incredible

18:30.446 --> 18:31.712
I have to show you.

18:31.747 --> 18:34.349
I learned how to
do a somersault.

18:35.716 --> 18:37.050
Did I do it?

18:37.101 --> 18:38.753
There he is.

18:38.787 --> 18:40.853
Crap.

18:40.887 --> 18:42.855
Daddy? What's going on here?

18:42.889 --> 18:46.092
Brian here has stolen something
very important from me.

18:46.127 --> 18:48.342
Give it to me, Brian.

18:48.376 --> 18:50.161
Lois, your father
has a cure for cancer,

18:50.195 --> 18:52.214
but he won't release it
because it's not profitable.

18:52.248 --> 18:53.713
So I stole it.

18:53.747 --> 18:55.247
Daddy, is this true?

18:55.282 --> 18:56.565
Yeah. So what?

18:56.600 --> 18:58.868
So I found the cure for cancer
and didn't announce it.

18:58.902 --> 19:00.535
Big deal.
I also took a dump today

19:00.569 --> 19:01.902
and didn't issue
a press release.

19:01.953 --> 19:03.705
Oh, that's interesting,
because I did.

19:03.739 --> 19:05.273
Good for Peter.

19:05.307 --> 19:07.875
It's about time the media
covered something positive.

19:07.909 --> 19:10.477
Daddy, you're the chairman
of a billion dollar company.

19:10.512 --> 19:12.178
You've got more
money than anyone

19:12.211 --> 19:13.845
could ever spend
in one lifetime.

19:13.880 --> 19:16.715
<i>Are you challenging me
to a Brewster's Millions?</i>

19:16.749 --> 19:18.716
Is money all that
you care about?

19:18.750 --> 19:20.986
What if somebody close
to you got cancer?

19:21.020 --> 19:23.386
What if... what if the
maid who raised you

19:23.421 --> 19:26.238
'cause your parents were too
wealthy and busy got cancer?

19:26.272 --> 19:27.439
You mean Mamie?

19:27.474 --> 19:29.476
Yeah.
What if Mamie got cancer?

19:29.510 --> 19:31.142
Mamie's not getting cancer!

19:31.176 --> 19:32.443
Or how about one of us?

19:32.478 --> 19:34.745
What if I got cancer
or, God forbid,

19:34.780 --> 19:36.582
one of your grandchildren?

19:36.616 --> 19:39.916
For once in your life,
do the right thing.

19:39.951 --> 19:41.518
All right, pumpkin.

19:41.552 --> 19:42.886
I'll do it.

19:42.920 --> 19:44.652
I'll do it for you.

19:44.687 --> 19:46.755
You will? You promise?

19:46.789 --> 19:47.822
Yes.

19:47.857 --> 19:49.258
I'll make the
announcement tomorrow.

19:49.292 --> 19:50.591
Oh, Daddy.

19:50.625 --> 19:51.692
Thank you so much.

19:51.726 --> 19:53.427
You're my hero.

19:53.461 --> 19:55.995
Oh, that's her father?

20:00.000 --> 20:01.200
Good afternoon. I'm Tom Tucker.

20:01.234 --> 20:02.933
Our top story:
a big announcement

20:02.968 --> 20:05.069
out of Pewterschmidt
Pharmaceuticals today.

20:07.441 --> 20:08.871
Company chairman
Carter Pewterschmidt

20:08.905 --> 20:10.573
released a statement
announcing the launch

20:10.608 --> 20:12.642
of a deodorant
for the developmentally disabled

20:12.676 --> 20:14.210
called Slow Stick.

20:14.245 --> 20:18.046
It's also edible because,
you know, those people...

20:18.081 --> 20:20.314
don't, uh... don't know...

20:20.350 --> 20:21.815
And now sports.

20:21.849 --> 20:23.383
That's it?
What the hell?

20:23.418 --> 20:25.419
Where's the cancer
cure announcement?

20:25.453 --> 20:28.287
That son of a bitch
went back on his word.

20:29.572 --> 20:30.873
CARTER:
Hello.

20:30.907 --> 20:33.174
Daddy, we're all sitting here
in front of the TV

20:33.209 --> 20:34.641
awaiting the big announcement.

20:34.676 --> 20:37.911
Remember the one that's
going to benefit all mankind?

20:37.946 --> 20:40.812
The one you promised to make
for me, your daughter?

20:40.847 --> 20:42.481
I lied.
(line clicks)

20:42.515 --> 20:44.050
Lois, you can't
stay mad at him.

20:44.084 --> 20:45.485
He's rich and successful.

20:45.519 --> 20:46.951
It doesn't excuse
what he did.

20:46.985 --> 20:48.102
It'll be fine.

20:48.136 --> 20:49.737
He'll just do
a magazine cover

20:49.771 --> 20:52.274
with a wink to who he is,
and all will be forgiven.

20:54.207 --> 20:55.675
Oh, Daddy.

20:55.700 --> 20:58.632
I know. If he's such a bad
guy, why's he on a magazine?

20:58.633 --> 21:59.633
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru